[17592186441429 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441429 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441429 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "95e16fd0-a1f9-576d-8004-2a0b51672b20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441430 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441430 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441430 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441430 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441430 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9f4b1bc8-53fc-58ca-a338-90cc2e17cebf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441431 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441431 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441431 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441431 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441431 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b7e72b22-0ede-5974-9f47-a89f67fcf4d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441432 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441432 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441432 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441432 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da43aee3-09a1-5940-8ad9-f3914ca15293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441433 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441433 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441433 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441433 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441433 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dbfacd55-1c0e-5862-bb80-6a63da7af696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441434 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441434 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441434 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441434 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441434 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186441415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441434 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441434 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441434 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186441392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441434 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f11adb4f-a6df-5572-a829-d227a29ca6f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441435 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441435 #edb/ref 1069 "q-165432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441435 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441435 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/db/dbf4056d9e2709bd959f4b8b79168941247af16ed1ae1b5dceb716cabe424830.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "165f408f-fdbf-512c-a0a1-ee39f4be587e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441435 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,4)$ and passes through the point $(2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,3),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-3)^{2} & =4p(3-4) \\\\ 1 & =4p(-1) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =-(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =-(0-4) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $1$ and $5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441436 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441436 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441436 #edb/ref 1069 "q-251823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441436 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441436 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/15/1500cb3f15e5a32229c861a7b5dcc19e06de0cd6757c8a872f5eb14ad6cb32de.png)\n\n**From top to bottom**, the **exact** values of the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4260057c-f969-5185-9095-04c56e87e760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441436 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,4)$ and passes through the point $(3,0).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-1,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4p(x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(3,0),$ we substitute $x=3$ and $y=0$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =4p(3+1) \\\\ 16 & =4p(4) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2+4 \\\\ y & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $6$ and  $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441437 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441437 #edb/ref 1069 "q-251439" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441437 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441437 #edb/ref 1071 "**From top to bottom**, the points of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}-12x-2y-8=0$ with the $y$-axis are $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of $A$ and $B.$\n\n$A(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$ and $\\,B(${{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441437 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48d7d71f-4c73-5bda-b5f4-b80929ee461c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441437 #edb/ref 1074 "When the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-12(0)-2y-8 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y-8 & =0 \\\\ (y+2)(y-4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the intersection points, from top to bottom, are $(0,4)$ and $(0,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441438 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441438 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73764" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441438 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441438 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point of intersection of the parabola\n$(y-9)^{2}=-3x$\nwith the $x$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "906c2725-0c67-5b11-96ab-a36547c9a017" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441438 #edb/ref 1074 "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-9)^{2} & =-3x \\\\ 81 & =-3x \\\\ x & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(-27,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441439 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441439 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441439 #edb/ref 1069 "q-251826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441439 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441439 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0e/0e95f9087b5b607f2d33d49c0f5db123eede589e53adc94747f3a33c1fe68f90.png)\n\n**From top to bottom**, the **exact** values of the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441439 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a25c3b40-7f3b-57d8-a341-b7f66aac34c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441439 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-8,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-6,1).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-8,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}=4p(x+8).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,1),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=1$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+1)^{2} & =4p(-6+8) \\\\ 4 & =4p(2) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(x+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(0+8) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ y & =\\pm 4-1 \\\\ y & =-5,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $3$ and  $-5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441440 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441440 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441440 #edb/ref 1069 "q-251434" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441440 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441440 #edb/ref 1071 "The parabola ${y}^{2}-3x+12y+36=0$ intersects the $x$-axis at the point$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441440 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af6bef8d-9bda-5865-8318-f03b8c8ed1c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441440 #edb/ref 1074 "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-3x+12y+36 & =0 \\\\ (0)^{2}-3x+12(0)+36 & =0 \\\\ -3x+36 & =0 \\\\ -3x & =-36 \\\\ x & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(12,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441441 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441441 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441441 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/19/19895cff608c6c9fce2598b235b5415ac2856ccf508f11df52545672918ad6f7.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441441 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "baa62b58-d974-5e44-ba9b-341e234529fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441441 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-2,-5)$ and passes through the point $(1,4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-2,-5)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}=4p(y+5).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(1,4),$ we substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+2)^{2} & =4p(4+5) \\\\ 9 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2} & =y+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+2)^{2} & =y+5 \\\\ 4 & =y+5 \\\\ y & =4-5 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441442 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441442 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the points of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-8(x-16)$ with the $y$-axis." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d5da328f-bab5-5074-ba79-45d8e6dffb79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441442 #edb/ref 1074 "When the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we set $x=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =-8(0-16) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =128 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{128} \\\\ y & =\\pm 8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $y$-axis at $(0,8\\sqrt{2})$ and $(0,-8\\sqrt{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
